WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:41.960
The “Spanish” flu pandemic was the single
worst disease episode in modern world history.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:48.170
In the space of eighteen months in 1918-1919,
its four epidemic waves killed tens of millions

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:53.370
of people around the globe, ravaging a population
that had already been exhausted by the mass

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.519
killing tactics of the War to End All Wars.

00:00:56.519 --> 00:01:00.929
In today’s video we will look at the Spanish
flu from three perspectives: first, how did

00:01:00.929 --> 00:01:05.500
it spread across the world and what was its
impact on the every day lives of ordinary

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:10.420
individuals; then, we will profile this invisible
killer, seeking to understand its origins

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:16.670
and murderous methods; finally, we will assess
the death toll it inflicted on the human species

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.159
and see what can we learn from the experience.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:21.619
And with that, let’s get into it.

00:01:21.619 --> 00:01:23.090
Part I: Long March of the Invisible Tyrant

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.480
[Production Note: In addition to the standard
chapter headings I have introduced headings

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.880
for Parts I, II and III to better organise
the three main themes.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.229
I suggest the ‘Part’ title cards are presented
as simple white text on a black background,

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:34.670
while the other title cards are displayed
as usual]

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.200
A Mysterious Origin Story

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:41.410
The term ‘Spanish’ is of course a misnomer,
it would be more appropriate to call it ‘The

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:46.720
Great Influenza Pandemic of 1918-1919’,
so I will use both terms.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.190
This epidemic did not originate in Spain,
it received this honorary citizenship only

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:56.800
because Spain was a neutral country in WWI
and its press was free from censorship to

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.580
report first about this mysterious disease.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.729
The origins of the pandemic lay elsewhere.

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:06.879
Most contemporary accounts place the first
outbreak at a US Army training base, Camp

00:02:06.879 --> 00:02:10.640
Funston, in Kansas, in mid-March 1918.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.180
It is here that this flu virus reaped its
first 48 victims.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.840
But we will never know for certain if that
was the true starting point.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.430
The Online Archive of California holds in
their Spanish Flu section a letter written

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.090
by a young recruit, Roy, from Camp Logan,
Texas.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.120
On the 1st of March 1918 he laments:

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.010
"We played another funeral today, one of the
lads from Company 1 died, I knew him real

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.010
well."

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:36.270
‘Another’ funeral on the 1st of March?

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:39.620
It seems like young soldiers had been dying
throughout February then.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:45.060
But the origins of the great flu can be traced
back even earlier than 1918.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:50.790
As early as 1916 and 1917 army camps in Étaples,
France and Aldershot, England had been ravaged

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.910
by an unknown respiratory disease.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:58.180
At the time it had been described as ‘purulent
bronchitis’, but its symptoms resembled

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.820
those of the Spanish flu.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.940
However, these outbreaks did not spread beyond
the two military bases and had died down by

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.760
April 1917.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:12.060
Historian Mark Humphries of Canada's Memorial
University of Newfoundland in 2014 proposed

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.930
another origin story.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:19.110
Humphries found archival evidence that a respiratory
illness that struck northern China in November

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:24.740
1917 was identified a year later by Chinese
health officials as identical to the Spanish

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:25.740
flu.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.660
From China, British officials organised the
transport of 94,000 labourers to the Western

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:35.080
Front, via North America, as part of an auxiliary
corps in charge of digging trenches and maintaining

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.080
supply lines.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.730
The labourers were sailed to Vancouver on
the Canadian West Coast, then loaded onto

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:45.190
trains to Halifax on the East Coast, from
which they could be sent to Europe.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:50.360
The press was forbidden from reporting on
these transports and the trains had been sealed:

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:55.349
both were meant as a precaution, to protect
the labourers from widespread anti-Chinese

00:03:55.349 --> 00:03:56.349
sentiment.

00:03:56.349 --> 00:04:00.739
But the un-intended effect may have been to
further contagion amongst the auxiliary’s

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:01.739
ranks.

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:06.250
Some 3,000 of them, while travelling through
Canada, were quarantined with flu-like symptoms.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:10.550
And of course, newspapers could not publish
a single word about it, meaning the infected

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.349
trains could continue their eastwards run.

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:18.321
The Chinese laborers arrived in southern England
by January 1918 and were sent to France, where

00:04:18.321 --> 00:04:24.030
a Hospital at Noyelles-sur-Mer recorded hundreds
of their deaths from respiratory illness.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:28.190
Whether because of the Chinese Labourers,
or US Army recruits, large movements of troops

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:31.819
were accountable for the rapid diffusion of
the virus.

00:04:31.819 --> 00:04:37.690
Between May and July the Great flu had spread
across virtually the whole of North and Central

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.150
America, Europe, and Asia.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:45.850
The Pravda in Russia wrote that ‘The Spanish
Lady’ had thrown a whole town, Murom, into

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:46.850
disarray.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:48.480
The Times of India reported that

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.230
"Nearly every house ... has some of its inmates
down with [influenza] fever and every office

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.340
is bewailing the absence of clerks,”

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:00.380
The epidemic had an obvious impact on the
war, which was still raging especially on

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:01.570
the Western Front.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.300
German General Erich Ludendorff complained
that

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:09.280
"It was a grievous business having to listen
every morning to the Chiefs of Staff’s recital

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.240
of the number of influenza cases, and their
complaints about the weakness of their troops.”

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.699
He would later even blame the failure of his
Spring Offensive on the virus.

00:05:18.699 --> 00:05:22.669
This was the First Wave, which seemed bad
at the time.

00:05:22.669 --> 00:05:24.880
And it actually was pretty bad.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.210
But it was nothing compared to what was to
come.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:35.210
Voices in The Second Wave

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:38.670
During the first sweep of the Spanish Lady,
the influenza virus probably went through

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.820
a mutation process which made it much deadlier.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.760
This enhanced killer made its first appearance
in late August of 1918, when the Second Wave

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:54.090
took off almost simultaneously from three
major wartime ports: Boston, USA; Brest, France;

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.130
and Freetown, Sierra Leone.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:01.100
Form the three ports, thousands of sailors,
soldiers and civilian travellers became vectors

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:02.200
for the virus.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:06.100
Aboard their ship, in a matter of weeks the
infected spread the disease across the four

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:10.919
corners of the Globe, with only a handful
of remote locations being spared the ordeal.

00:06:10.919 --> 00:06:15.410
The Second Wave dragged down throughout the
whole of Autumn and into early Winter of 1919.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:21.080
The end of the war, on the 11th of November
1918, did not mitigate the onslaught, arguably

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:26.470
it made it worse: millions of soldiers were
demobilised, hundreds of thousands of PoW’s

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:30.600
returned home, carrying an unwanted stowaway
inside their lungs.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:35.950
A whole generation of young men had been decimated
by artillery, weaponised gas, machine gun

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:42.020
fire … and those who survived were likely
to die because of a single droplet of saliva.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:47.850
It took only one single virus, inside a single
droplet out of 40,000 ejected by a single

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:51.990
sneeze … and like that, you were infected.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:57.360
The feeling of the fragility of life in those
times is captured by the every-day correspondence

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:58.360
of ordinary people.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:02.669
In September 1918 a Post Office worker in
Plymouth, Massachusetts wrote to his wife

00:07:02.669 --> 00:07:08.509
about the gruelling conditions he worked in
to ensure a continuing service, amidst the

00:07:08.509 --> 00:07:10.590
constant death caused by the flu:

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:15.010
“I have some more very bad news to write
you and I guess if things keep on that by

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.920
the end of the year there will be nobody left
in town.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:23.060
Harold Ashley died yesterday …Harold will
be buried just as soon as his body arrives

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:24.400
and there will be no funeral

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:30.130
On Friday we had seven funerals and Saturday
about as many, so you see what we are up against.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:33.600
In Brockton three days last week there was
one every hour”

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:38.320
On the 11th of October, a recruit in training
in Michigan wrote to a friend that in his

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:39.320
camp

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.389
’50 die every day from it’

00:07:41.389 --> 00:07:47.100
From the 16th to the 27th of October, a series
of letter from Mrs Alma Sr to her daughter

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:51.360
Alma Jr show how unbreakable can a mother’s
love be.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:54.662
Writing from Rochester, New York, Mrs Alma
worries that her daughter is safe and healthy,

00:07:54.662 --> 00:07:57.810
in her college in Ontario, Canada.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:01.410
Some of the motherly advice may resonate with
what you hear these days:

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:05.470
“Was just wondering how you were getting
in these days of epidemic - in the Country

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:10.180
it is fierce and so many dying …
Over here is something awful people are dying

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:13.759
and it's awful …
Don't worry or fret because you only can walk

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:18.870
in the yard - be glad you can do that … keep
in fresh air all you can … and keep warm

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:23.600
put your bathrobe on at night … hot lemonade
at night is excellent for influenza.”

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.560
Mrs Alma then laments that she cannot help
her other daughter with her little baby, Jane

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.600
because she is invalided at home, and that
it is

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.990
“impossible to get nurses”

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:36.270
While worrying about the well-being of her
daughters and doing what she could to help

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.320
her granddaughter, Mrs Alma was ill with cancer.

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:43.500
Maybe unable to access medical care earlier
because of the pandemic, she was eventually

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:48.220
hospitalised on the 27th of October, and died
on the 6th of November.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:52.410
On the 31st of October, an official at the
Mayor office of San Francisco took the initiative

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:57.120
to notify his counterpart in Santa Barbara,
about effective public health measures in

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:58.120
his city.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:02.040
“Sending this for your information because
I have seen the whole terrible effect of epidemic

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:07.450
here, because masks have saved untold suffering
and many deaths, and because Santa Barbara

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:08.990
is my old home city"

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:12.790
The measure in place was simply the universal
wearing of masks.

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:17.540
Three days after the use became general, new
cases in San Francisco dropped by 50%, but

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:19.990
the daily death toll was still above 100.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:23.650
On the other side of the pond, London was
being stricken as hard as New York State,

00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:25.350
California and Ontario.

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:28.220
On the 28th of October 1918, one Mrs Bennett
wrote

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:32.790
“Things here are in a terrible state, this
new flu … taking people off as they walk

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:33.990
along the streets.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:38.649
In fact, the undertakers can’t turn the
coffins out or bury the people quick enough.

00:09:38.649 --> 00:09:42.010
There's families of 6 &amp; 7 in one house lay
dead.”

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:45.850
On the same day, an American soldier stationed
in France wrote to his mother, reassuring

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:50.290
her that the situation where he was stationed
was not as bad as it was at another base,

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:51.290
Camp Sherman.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.420
He also mentioned that

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:56.460
“The French people have a way of treating
[the flu], they make some kind of tea of some

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:57.590
kind of leaves....”

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:02.270
Unfortunately, he did not provide the recipe.

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:06.490
The Last Waves

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:10.410
Another series of letters from an American
private provides a good example of acquired

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:11.410
immunity.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:17.070
Martin Aloysius Culhane, known as ‘Al’,
was stationed at Camp Forrest, Georgia, form

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:19.660
where he wrote to his brother and to his best
friend.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:23.951
While he mourned the death of his ‘bunkie’,
Thomas, he was in relative high spirit: he

00:10:23.951 --> 00:10:27.310
had survived the Great pandemic, not once,
but twice!

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:30.040
“I am still in quarantine but will be released
today.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:34.529
I am feeling great and the two day's rest
has done me a world of good”

00:10:34.529 --> 00:10:37.529
His main concern was that while in quarantine
he had been

00:10:37.529 --> 00:10:39.560
“neglecting the ladies”.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:44.810
So he asked his friend Cliff to check on Ursula,
Ella, Ida, Mary Rose, Mary English and Mary

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:45.810
Anne.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:49.620
So, was ‘Al’ simply lucky, in love and
in health?

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:53.760
There is strong evidence suggesting that exposure
to previous waves of the Spanish flu could

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:58.610
generate acquired immunity in those who had
been infected, but had survived.

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:04.269
A research led by Dr JM Barry from Tulane
and Xavier Universities, Louisiana, highlighted

00:11:04.269 --> 00:11:08.680
how the First Wave of the Spanish Flu was
characterized by a high number of cases, but

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:11.130
had a lower fatality rate than the Second
Wave.

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:14.980
By looking at medical records of US soldiers,
he estimated that the fatality rate during

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:18.130
the lethal Autumn sweep was 4.7%.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:20.040
In the Spring, it had been only 1.1%.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:25.190
However, the deadly barrage of the Second
Wave had largely spared those who had been

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:26.990
previously exposed in the Spring!

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:29.250
The case of Camp Dodge illustrates this perfectly:

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:33.550
Here, one regiment of troops had experienced
the First Wave, while another regiment had

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:34.550
escaped it.

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:39.730
Among those exposed in the Spring, only 6.6%
became sick with flu in the Autumn.

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:45.500
In contrast, among the troops not exposed
in the spring, a staggering 48.5% fell ill

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:46.500
during the Second Wave!

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:52.080
Now, the witness accounts and the research
mentioned so far may refer to America or Europe,

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.279
but this should not give the impression that
other continents were spared.

00:11:56.279 --> 00:11:59.029
Let’s go back to September 1918.

00:11:59.029 --> 00:12:03.380
This is when Indian troops who had served
on the Western front returned home.

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:09.140
The first outbreak took place in Bombay, where
mortality rate was more than 5% of all population.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:15.050
The Spanish Lady had now access to access
to a huge new territory, densely populated,

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:17.779
serviced by a large railway network.

00:12:17.779 --> 00:12:19.100
The perfect storm, in other words.

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:23.860
In just a month, the flu had spread to Sri
Lanka and to the Northern provinces, reaping

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:29.870
an estimated 14 to 18 Million lives, the highest
death toll recorded in a single country.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:34.360
Also in September, a troop transport ship
sailed off Bombay to Mombasa, Kenya.

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:36.560
Of course, the virus was a stow away.

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:41.430
The infection spread to members of the Kenyan
Carrier Corps, who were just about to be demobilised.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:45.520
As they returned home, they carried the contagion
inland, adding to the death toll caused by

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:50.060
the other two major African outbreaks, originating
in Sierra Leone and South Africa.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:54.209
Most of Africa had been spared the First Wave,
meaning that the Autumn burst had cruelly

00:12:54.209 --> 00:12:59.230
scorched through the continent: towns like
Kimberley, Addis Ababa, and Windhoek lost

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:02.110
over 4 percent of their populations to flu.

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:07.279
The bulk of the victims were aged between
18 and 40: so, the effect on society was disastrous

00:13:07.279 --> 00:13:10.970
in terms of loss of labour, reproductive capacity,
and family structures.

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:16.019
It was a sudden demographic, social and economic
catastrophe without precedent.

00:13:16.019 --> 00:13:19.280
Back in India, by November the rate of contagion
had largely subsided.

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:23.760
It is speculated that this was due to the
onset of the monsoon season, as humid climates

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.899
are not favourable to flu viruses.

00:13:25.899 --> 00:13:29.970
The march of the invisible tyrant, nonetheless,
continued eastwards, reaching China.

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:35.600
Until now, the majority of the victims worldwide
had been aged 15 to 34, an age bracket unusual

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:37.070
for influenza pandemics.

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:40.790
We’ll find out later why this group was
particularly targeted.

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:45.930
In China, the Second Wave preyed on an even
younger cohort, children aged 11 to 15.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:50.750
Overall, however, the mortality rate here
was much lower compared to India and other

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:51.750
Countries.

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:54.490
Researchers from the Hong Kong Institute of
Chinese Medicine theorize that traditional

00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:57.200
Chinese medicine may have played a part.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:01.470
Remedies put in place by local government
included spraying houses with limewater, or

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:04.320
burning rhubarb and root stalks of the Atractylodes
flower to disinfect the air.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.450
For prevention, villagers were advised to
drink mung bean soup and rock sugar.

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:12.550
Another explanation is the one hinted at earlier:
since the pandemic had originated there, Chinese

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:15.170
citizens had already developed an immunity
to it.

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:20.910
By the early months of 1919 the rage of the
pandemic was relatively tamed, and I stress

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:21.910
relatively.

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:26.000
The early Spring of the year saw a third wave
making the rounds throughout the globe.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:31.450
Although it did not match the speed, reach
and virulence of the Autumn wave, it was still

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:32.450
deadly.

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:36.010
On March 5th 1919, a young Canadian man, Harold,
wrote to a friend in Illinois:

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:40.060
"There are an awful lot of people dying just
now on account of the flu.

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:45.000
A little girl next door was buried today and
there is a lady up the street that is pretty

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.389
sick and my little sister is just getting
over it …

00:14:48.389 --> 00:14:50.490
So I guess it is pretty near my turn …”

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:53.550
We don’t know if Harold made it or not.

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:57.889
The third sweep was followed by a Fourth Wave,
less severe, which lingered on in locations

00:14:57.889 --> 00:15:01.060
such as Scandinavia, the South Atlantic and
Sicily.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:06.269
It is still debated whether this was a mutated
strain of the original virus, or a new one

00:15:06.269 --> 00:15:07.269
altogether.

00:15:07.269 --> 00:15:11.190
One of the last documented victims of the
Fourth Wave was a little girl from Palermo,

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:12.649
Rosalia Lombardo.

00:15:12.649 --> 00:15:16.950
She had been born on the 13th of December
1918, at the height of the Autumn flu in Europe,

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:20.970
and passed away aged only two, on the 6th
of December 1920.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:25.519
Her father, aggrieved by such a sudden and
meaningless death, asked for her body to be

00:15:25.519 --> 00:15:27.850
preserved as if she were still asleep.

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:33.410
One century later, Rosalia can still be seen
sleeping in the Capuchin Catacombs of Palermo.

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:38.130
This unwitting memorial to the Great Pandemic
is now known as ‘the most beautiful mummy

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:39.889
in Europe’.

00:15:39.889 --> 00:15:48.250
Part II: Profiling the Killer

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:51.760
One of a Kind

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:57.261
At a high level, flu viruses are divided into
four types, from A to D. And type A’s can

00:15:57.261 --> 00:16:05.180
be divided into further sub-categories, depending
on the subtypes of two proteins present on

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:09.180
their surface: hemagglutinin, ‘H’, and
neuraminidase, ‘N’.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:14.769
The influenza virus responsible for the 1918
pandemic was identified as being a Type A,

00:16:14.769 --> 00:16:16.769
H1N1 subtype.

00:16:16.769 --> 00:16:20.500
Viruses of the A, H1N1 type are actually quite
common amongst humans.

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:26.089
But we’ll find out later what made this
specific virus so special – and so lethal.

00:16:26.089 --> 00:16:32.000
A 2001 study suggested that the 1918 virus
may have originated in swine hosts, before

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:33.060
moving onto humans.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:38.670
But another investigation in 2005 suggested
a more likely source: birds.

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:43.920
The enteric tracts of waterfowl such as ducks
and geese are known to be the reservoirs for

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:46.230
all known influenza A viruses.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:51.590
For those of you not au fait with physiology
or anatomy, the enteric tract is the nervous

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:54.110
system that regulates digestion.

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:59.610
Within the original host, let’s say a duck,
the virus may mutate over time: either independently,

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:03.540
or by recombination with a gene segment from
a different virus.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:09.029
Now that the pathogen is mutated, it is ready
to jump species, usually from avian to mammal,

00:17:09.029 --> 00:17:13.470
and attack a new type of cells, for example
those lining the lungs.

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:17.650
This is almost certainly what happened to
the Spanish flu strain, and I should stress

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:18.650
almost.

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:24.029
In two separate studies of 1999 and 2005,
Oxford University and the Atlanta Centre for

00:17:24.029 --> 00:17:28.909
Disease Control were able to reconstruct the
genome of the original virus.

00:17:28.909 --> 00:17:34.129
What they found is that the 1918 influenza
was composed of 8 gene segments, all distinct

00:17:34.129 --> 00:17:40.389
from any of the hundreds of both avian and
mammalian viruses collected since 1917.

00:17:40.389 --> 00:17:42.749
This … little fella… was literally one
of a kind!

00:17:42.749 --> 00:17:47.549
This unique quality made it impossible to
ascertain who or what could have been the

00:17:47.549 --> 00:17:52.479
original host of the virus, and how it had
evolved into its lethal final form.

00:17:52.479 --> 00:17:57.489
Another clue: normally, outbreaks of viruses
with an avian origin are preceded by large

00:17:57.489 --> 00:18:02.659
die-off of poultry or other bird population
… but this was not the case for the 1918

00:18:02.659 --> 00:18:03.659
pandemic.

00:18:03.659 --> 00:18:08.419
The initial animal host, the patient alpha,
is still unidentified.

00:18:08.419 --> 00:18:11.690
Modus Operandi

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:21.340
One thing was clear about the 1918 H1N1 virus:
its virulence and pathogenicity were unrivalled,

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:24.820
compared to other viruses which both preceded
it and followed it.

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:26.740
To clarify these points:

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:32.450
Pathogenicity is defined as the ability of
an infectious agent to cause disease or damage

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:33.450
in a host.

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:37.510
Virulence, on the other hand, is a measure
of the severity of said disease.

00:18:37.510 --> 00:18:40.100
A microorganism can be pathogenic, or not.

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:44.539
If it is pathogenic, its virulence can vary
from mild to fatal.

00:18:44.539 --> 00:18:47.020
Let’s look at pathogenicity first.

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:51.809
Contemporary, ‘normal’ flu viruses require
the presence of the enzyme trypsin in their

00:18:51.809 --> 00:18:54.269
human host, in order to replicate.

00:18:54.269 --> 00:18:59.669
The 1918 virus instead could proliferate even
in the absence of trypsin, making it more

00:18:59.669 --> 00:19:01.549
pathogenic than other viruses.

00:19:01.549 --> 00:19:06.779
The Spanish flu also displayed a unique capacity
to proliferate at high rates inside human

00:19:06.779 --> 00:19:09.499
bronchial cells – and that explains its
virulence.

00:19:09.499 --> 00:19:14.639
Little by little, a picture of the killer
was emerging, thanks to modern researchers

00:19:14.639 --> 00:19:18.350
in the role of crime scene investigators,
or criminal profilers.

00:19:18.350 --> 00:19:25.359
The next step was to understand how Mr Type
A H1N1 of the 1918 class actually killed:

00:19:25.359 --> 00:19:27.009
what was its modus operandi.

00:19:27.009 --> 00:19:33.159
Enter Dr David Morens of the National Institute
of Allergy and Infectious Diseases in Maryland.

00:19:33.159 --> 00:19:39.970
In 2007 he reviewed autopsy records for influenza
casualties in the 1918-1919 period and identified

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:44.619
two overlapping syndromes which caused the
death of the infected hosts.

00:19:44.619 --> 00:19:50.070
In at least 85% to 90% of cases, patients
suffered from an acute viral infection, that

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:55.080
spread down the respiratory trait, causing
severe tissue damage inside the lungs.

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:58.899
The damaged tissue was then invaded by a so-called
secondary invasion.

00:19:58.899 --> 00:20:04.909
Not viruses this time, but bacteria: pathogens
like Streptococcus Pneumoniae or Staphylococcus

00:20:04.909 --> 00:20:10.049
aureus would invade the damaged lungs, causing
cases of aggressive bronchopneumonia.

00:20:10.049 --> 00:20:13.820
In other words, the killing virus did not
act alone, but had accomplices.

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:18.549
The actions of these cohorts eventually led
to severe pulmonary haemorrhage and necrosis

00:20:18.549 --> 00:20:22.309
– or tissue death – within the bronchi.

00:20:22.309 --> 00:20:27.960
The remaining 10 to 15% of fatal cases suffered
from ARDS, or ‘acute respiratory distress—like

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:28.960
syndrome’.

00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:33.029
Patients with this syndrome could be easily
identified by their peculiar blue-grey facial

00:20:33.029 --> 00:20:35.710
discoloration: a typical sign of drowning.

00:20:35.710 --> 00:20:39.749
Because that’s essentially what was happening:
the virus caused the pulmonary blood vessels

00:20:39.749 --> 00:20:44.369
to exude huge amounts of thin, watery droplets,
which clogged the lung tissue.

00:20:44.369 --> 00:20:49.440
In other words: these patients were drowning
in a liquid created by their own bodies.

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:53.639
Now that we have clarified the modus operandi
of this killer, we have to understand its

00:20:53.639 --> 00:20:55.659
peculiar choice of victims.

00:20:55.659 --> 00:21:00.239
Normally flu epidemics claim most of their
victims among the elderly, but the Spanish

00:21:00.239 --> 00:21:06.359
Flu had a clear predilection for a younger
demographic, those aged between 15 and 34.

00:21:06.359 --> 00:21:11.419
A lower than expected mortality among the
elderly may have been explained by acquired

00:21:11.419 --> 00:21:12.419
immunity.

00:21:12.419 --> 00:21:16.590
Individuals aged 40 and over, may have been
exposed to one of the three flu pandemics

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:22.009
of the 19th Century, in 1830, 1847 or 1889.

00:21:22.009 --> 00:21:27.369
Although not everybody agrees: unlike the
1918 strain, these previous viruses did not

00:21:27.369 --> 00:21:31.019
cause aggressive bronchopneumonia nor lung
haemorrhage.

00:21:31.019 --> 00:21:35.580
We may have more convincing answers explaining
why the death rate was higher among the young

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:36.580
ones.

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:40.440
Dr Robert Snelgrove from Imperial College
London, among others, has theorised the notion

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:43.039
of the so-called ‘cytokine storm’.

00:21:43.039 --> 00:21:47.870
Cytokines are proteins released by cells to
interact with each other and to trigger specific

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:48.870
processes.

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:53.470
For example, internal inflammation – a common
reaction of the body against infections.

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:58.409
A younger organism would naturally release
more cytokines than and elderly one.

00:21:58.409 --> 00:22:03.419
In the case of the Spanish flu, the cells
of infected younger patients released a deleterious

00:22:03.419 --> 00:22:09.519
excess of cytokines, leading to excessive
inflammation of the lungs, and more importantly,

00:22:09.519 --> 00:22:10.519
tissue necrosis.

00:22:10.519 --> 00:22:15.469
Ironically, younger, fitter organisms deployed
a stronger reaction to protect themselves

00:22:15.469 --> 00:22:19.999
– a reaction which eventually claimed their
lives.

00:22:19.999 --> 00:22:25.549
Part III: Assessing the Damage

00:22:25.549 --> 00:22:28.879
A Pale Rider

00:22:28.879 --> 00:22:34.109
Shortly after the last wave had died down,
the scientific community tried to take stock

00:22:34.109 --> 00:22:36.450
of the magnitude of the disaster.

00:22:36.450 --> 00:22:40.559
American bacteriologist Edwin Oakes Jordan
was one of the first to calculate the death

00:22:40.559 --> 00:22:45.190
toll, which he estimated to be in the vicinity
of 21.5 million.

00:22:45.190 --> 00:22:48.070
This number in itself is staggering.

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:52.960
Compare it to the casualties of the Great
War: about 10 Million dead soldiers, killed

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:54.759
over a period of four years and a half.

00:22:54.759 --> 00:22:58.749
The Spanish Flu killed twice as many people
over little more than a year.

00:22:58.749 --> 00:23:01.299
Now let’s compare it to the world’s population
in 1918.

00:23:01.299 --> 00:23:04.590
At that time there were 1.8 Billion on the
planet.

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:07.690
The Spanish flu killed 1.2% of them.

00:23:07.690 --> 00:23:13.509
If we apply that ratio to today’s population
we would have a death toll of more than 93

00:23:13.509 --> 00:23:14.649
million.

00:23:14.649 --> 00:23:20.389
Oakes Jordan’s estimate stood for decades,
but it was later challenged as being too low.

00:23:20.389 --> 00:23:26.639
First, a 1986 publication calculated the mortality
in India alone to be at 18 million.

00:23:26.639 --> 00:23:32.019
Subsequently, in 1991 authors David Patterson
and Gerald Pyle proposed another estimate,

00:23:32.019 --> 00:23:36.489
placing the death toll between 25 and 40 million
victims.

00:23:36.489 --> 00:23:38.450
Was that the definitive number?

00:23:38.450 --> 00:23:39.580
Probably not.

00:23:39.580 --> 00:23:45.889
A 2002 study by Dr Niall Johnson and Dr Juergen
Mueller suggested that previous numbers may

00:23:45.889 --> 00:23:50.799
have been underestimated due to common issues
such as missing records, misdiagnosis or death

00:23:50.799 --> 00:23:53.259
certificates compiled by non-medical personnel.

00:23:53.259 --> 00:23:58.970
Not only that: deaths among indigenous populations
in colonial dominions may have been overlooked,

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:02.450
ignored or simply not reported by colonial
authorities.

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:06.629
Also, most of the reports collected at the
time of the pandemic referred to deaths taking

00:24:06.629 --> 00:24:10.299
place during the major wave of Autumn-Winter.

00:24:10.299 --> 00:24:14.250
Influenza mortality before and after that
wave may have been ignored or reported under

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:16.659
other causes of death, such as pneumonia.

00:24:16.659 --> 00:24:20.999
All in all, considering all these instances
of under-reporting, Johnson and Mueller placed

00:24:20.999 --> 00:24:25.649
their estimate in a range from 50 up to 100
million dead.

00:24:25.649 --> 00:24:29.039
Let’s stop for a second to consider that.

00:24:29.039 --> 00:24:30.309
100 million dead.

00:24:30.309 --> 00:24:33.879
The entire population of the US at that time
was just 108 million.

00:24:33.879 --> 00:24:37.539
The whole of Latin America only had 57 million
inhabitants.

00:24:37.539 --> 00:24:43.320
Sometimes numbers may not be enough to represent
the sheer scale of a tragedy, so think about

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:44.320
it this way:

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:50.299
in a matter of few months, one USA or two
Latin Americas had been completely wiped out

00:24:50.299 --> 00:24:55.809
by a microscopic being which had likely originated
in the bowels of a duck.

00:24:55.809 --> 00:24:59.599
In all honesty, the more we researched the
death toll, the more we found conflicting

00:24:59.599 --> 00:25:03.749
accounts, ranging from the top count of 100
million, down to the no less tragic 18 million.

00:25:03.749 --> 00:25:07.950
We will likely never know how many people
did not get to see their next year because

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:12.340
of the Great killer of 1918 and 1919.

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:17.979
A Tragedy of the Unborn

00:25:17.979 --> 00:25:22.619
In our grim research we also came across an
even bleaker notion: how many victims didn’t

00:25:22.619 --> 00:25:24.509
even get to see their first day of life.

00:25:24.509 --> 00:25:28.640
In other words: the impact of the Great Influenza
on unborn children.

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:33.869
Dr Bloom-Feshbach of Mount Sinai School of
Medicine, New York, and her team have examined

00:25:33.869 --> 00:25:39.039
the relationship between influenza and birth
rates during the Great pandemic in the US,

00:25:39.039 --> 00:25:40.559
Denmark, Sweden, and Norway.

00:25:40.559 --> 00:25:45.840
The analysis showed a decline in birth rates
for all examined populations in spring of

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:46.840
1919.

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:51.869
This decline represented a 15% drop below
baseline levels recorded before the pandemic.

00:25:51.869 --> 00:25:56.320
This ‘natality depression’ reached its
maximum levels about 6 months after the pandemic

00:25:56.320 --> 00:25:58.029
peak of the previous autumn.

00:25:58.029 --> 00:26:02.679
This suggested that the decline in birth could
be attributed to a surge in first trimester

00:26:02.679 --> 00:26:06.809
miscarriages, which affected about 10% of
all women who were pregnant at the peak of

00:26:06.809 --> 00:26:07.809
the pandemic.

00:26:07.809 --> 00:26:12.700
The conclusion of Bloom-Feshbach and team
is that the virus was directly responsible

00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:14.279
for the miscarriages.

00:26:14.279 --> 00:26:17.580
And the 10% incidence rate may have been an
underestimation.

00:26:17.580 --> 00:26:23.960
As they point out, a 1919 report of 1350 pregnant
women with influenza, showed how more than

00:26:23.960 --> 00:26:26.399
a quarter of them had miscarried.

00:26:26.399 --> 00:26:30.080
The concept of so many babies not even being
born is hard to accept.

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:35.169
But the same study revealed another finding,
which shows that regardless of the disaster,

00:26:35.169 --> 00:26:38.899
our species is always capable of bouncing
back up.

00:26:38.899 --> 00:26:42.049
The dip in birth rates in spring of 1919 was
followed by

00:26:42.049 --> 00:26:49.039
‘a compensatory natality increase in late
autumn 1919 and early spring 1920, when natality

00:26:49.039 --> 00:26:51.999
significantly exceeded the expected rates.’

00:26:51.999 --> 00:26:56.499
The resurgence of births confirmed that fertility
was not permanently impacted.

00:26:56.499 --> 00:27:01.029
Humanity could slowly rebuild itself, lick
the wounds inflicted by the double tragedy

00:27:01.029 --> 00:27:04.929
of the Great War and the Great Pandemic, and
slowly return to normal.

00:27:04.929 --> 00:27:10.580
Return to a daily life, and a historical period
that was going to be devoid of war, famine

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:18.389
and disease, at least according to some leaders
… a period which turned out to be only a

00:27:18.389 --> 00:27:19.840
20-year truce.

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:22.739
But that’s another story.

00:27:22.739 --> 00:27:25.639
Could It Happen Today?

00:27:25.639 --> 00:27:30.779
Back in 2004, a team at the Harvard School
of Public Health had modelled the transmissibility

00:27:30.779 --> 00:27:36.950
patterns of the 1918 virus, more precisely
they calculated its reproductive number, ‘R’,

00:27:36.950 --> 00:27:39.960
in relation to its serial interval, ‘v’.

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:44.570
The ‘R’ number tells how many secondary
patients are produced by each primary patient.

00:27:44.570 --> 00:27:49.120
The ‘v’ value is the average time – expressed
in days - between a primary and secondary

00:27:49.120 --> 00:27:50.120
case.

00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:55.590
The team evaluated that the 1918 virus had
an ‘R0’ value of 2 to 4.

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:59.840
In lay terms: an infected patient could infect
four more patients in less than a day.

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:04.049
This may sound like a high rate of contagion,
however the researchers pointed out that this

00:28:04.049 --> 00:28:08.089
was not that high, in relation to other influenza
subtypes.

00:28:08.089 --> 00:28:12.960
In their estimation, a similar pandemic could
be prevented by vaccinating or administering

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:16.010
antiviral prophylaxis to 50–75% of the population.

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:18.070
Sounds easy, right?

00:28:18.070 --> 00:28:22.799
Well, this depends on how well prepared a
national health system is, when it comes to

00:28:22.799 --> 00:28:24.490
facing such emergencies.

00:28:24.490 --> 00:28:29.330
Stockpiles of vaccines and antivirals may
or may not be enough – or these drugs may

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:33.469
not be available at all, still progressing
through the early stages of a clinical trial.

00:28:33.469 --> 00:28:38.890
There is the added problem that flu viruses
may be transmitted before the onset of defining

00:28:38.890 --> 00:28:40.029
signs and symptoms.

00:28:40.029 --> 00:28:44.460
In other words: people infect each other even
before they appear to be sick.

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:46.610
So, what is left to do?

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:49.109
I will quote directly from the clinical paper:

00:28:49.109 --> 00:28:53.479
“This implies that measures that generally
reduce contacts between persons, regardless

00:28:53.479 --> 00:28:59.039
of infection status, may be our most powerful
protection against a pandemic until adequate

00:28:59.039 --> 00:29:04.869
vaccine and antiviral medicines can be produced,
at which point mass-vaccination and prophylaxis

00:29:04.869 --> 00:29:07.849
may be more effective than targeted approaches.”

00:29:07.849 --> 00:29:10.219
I hope that by the time you watch this video
the current Covid-19 pandemic is a thing of

00:29:10.219 --> 00:29:11.219
the past.

00:29:11.219 --> 00:29:13.280
If it’s not, well I hope that this message
resonated with you.

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:16.299
You have heard it a million times, we all
have heard it.

00:29:16.299 --> 00:29:21.259
Until we are through this emergency, staying
at home is our most powerful protection.

00:29:21.259 --> 00:29:26.019
Stay at home, stay safe, and thank you for
watching.

